er it is  telecommunications what we see our  merger after merger after merger and  fewer and fewer corporations run by  smaller and smaller groups of  individuals who control more and more of  the market and when these people make a  mistake those are mistakes heard all  over the world which affect the lives of  billions of people and I think that  laissez-faire attitude that mr.  Greenspan his colleagues have had  created are leading us into a very very  dangerous situation now my question for  mr. Greenspan is a very simple one in  the United States of America today you  have one man who owns more wealth than  100 million Americans in the world you  have 225 people who own more wealth than  almost half the world's population does  that concern you do you think that is  just and not only in terms of justice  and morality aren't you concerned about  the economic instability that can occur  when so few have so much power and when  mistakes are made such as with long-term  capital looking at the economic  repercussions of what a few people can  do does that concern you mr. Greenspan  well if you're asking me what I prefer  that there was less concentration of  income less concentration of wealth I  would say yes generally I would and I  would would because I believe that  democratic institutions tend to function  best when you have fairly even  distributions the question I think which  is crucial to your i should say the  issue which is crucial to your question  is what would you do about it and to  what extent would that improve the state  of being of the world at large and the  individuals who don't have that wealth  it's not by any means clear to me that  if you were somehow to take these 225  individuals and merely indicate to them  that they no longer have any wealth and  you put them away on a desert island  that the state of the rest of the world  would be improved in the slightest if  you're asking me to individuals who have  significant power and when they make  mistakes does it have consequences to  others that's always been the case  throughout history I think we ought to  instead of looking at what we are what  we have now as some incredibly corrupt  unequal unethical system is to try to  look at what the United States has  become relative to what used to exist  100 to 300 years ago I would say to you  that the standard of living at the  average American is dramatically higher  that the average American is far better  off than at any time in our history and  I would suggest to you that that  particular state would not be improved  by confiscating the wealth of the every  billionaire in the United States someone  suggested that but by the way you  mentioned the moment ago about how I  believe I I remembering what you said  that the United States has the highest  standard of living in the world this is  a country we have 43 million of  Americans with no health insurance we  have a situation where the typical  married couple family work 247 more  hours in 1996 that in 1989 where real  wages have declined in the real last 20  years but be that I made my second  question mr. Chairman I think it's  important for our banking committee  because this issue ties into HR 10 now  mr. Greenspan as you know the Fed  recently approved the acquisition of  citicorp by travels we all know that  citibank is heavily involved in  developing countries developing markets  a highly troubled sector to say the  least travelers reported large losses  during the recent market turmoil and is  now reported to be a major investor in  term capital why shouldn't we be  concerned that allowing combinations  like Citigroup increases overall risk  for financial institutions rather than  decreasing risk which takes us through  the whole issue of HR 10 and making it  easier for large banks to merge with  other types of companies mr. Greenspan  well first of all let me just say we  process the application according to the  law according to what the statute under  which those applications require we made  judgments in all regard we made  judgments about the nature of the merger  and when we believe that all  requirements of the law were met we  approved it as we are required to do if  you're asking the broader question about  these larger institutions one of the  reasons why for example we after  thinking about it for a while decided  that the basket the ability of  institutions to have very substantial  interplay between the non-financial part  of the economy and the financial part  which would be significantly enhanced  under for some of the early versions of  HR 10 why we opposed and in retrospect I  think correctly HR 10 has passed by the  House of Representatives in my judgment  was a very good bill the issue of making  certain that all of the new powers are  required to be financed in the  marketplace at competitive rates and  therefore through an affiliate of a  holding company as distinct from as was  originally discussed in many different  versions of the bill  through subsidiaries of the bank i would  say that one of the reasons why we are  very much concerned that indeed all of  these powers be in the holding companies  is that we are worried about the issue  of subsidies coming out of the bank into  the subsidiary of the bank and being  used in a non-competitive manner to  employ expansion of powers which are in  HR 10 so the answer is yes I am I am a  little concerned about some of the size  of these various institutions and I  think it's very important that we make  certain that we don't subsidize them  Thank You mr. grits mr. Chairman I would  just conclude by suggesting I think this  isn't important here and I think it lays  the groundwork for future hearings in  which we should take a hard look at the  degree of concentration of ownership in  this country and the implication for our  national and international economy thank  you I thank the gentleman I would only  add as the jungle is noted that in 